arm64: dts: h713: drop the 1416 MHz OPP, it corrupts memory

Running the CPU at 1416 MHz corrupts kernel memory under sustained load.
Removing the OPP fixes it, and the vendor never uses that point at any
voltage.

This closes an investigation that spent a long time in the wrong subsystem
(docs/vaapi-scope.md). The symptom was that mpv rendering on the GPU to the
panel killed the board in 40-90 s, with a different victim every run -- a
task_struct's cpu_context.sp with its top half zeroed, a pool_workqueue in
pwq_dec_nr_in_flight, the CCU's ioremapped register pointer, a userspace
libavcodec thread faulting in the same microsecond as the kernel. It was
chased through cedrus, the IOMMU, panfrost's dma-buf import path and CMA,
all of which were excluded by experiment. The clue that redirected it was
that two consecutive crashes landed *inside* the CPU DVFS path
(sugov_work -> __cpufreq_driver_target -> clk_set_rate ->
ccu_pll_notifier_cb -> ccu_helper_wait_for_lock), which had been dismissed
as a hot path, and that `ffff` -> `faef` in a corrupted pointer is
XOR 0x0510 -- three flipped bits, not an overwrite.

Measured on the bench, mpv --vo=gpu to the panel via
tools/video/soak-display-only.sh, GPU at ~490 IRQ/s and the panel at 59
commits/s throughout:

  ceiling 1416 MHz   crash at 40 s, ~60-90 s, 75 s          (n=3)
  ceiling 1296 MHz   20 min clean, 581k GPU IRQs, khz=1296000 seen 16x
  ceiling 1200 MHz   20 min clean, 578k GPU IRQs

Frequency *transitions* are not the problem: the 1200 and 1296 arms ran
schedutil with transitions happening throughout. Only the top point matters.

The vendor does not use 1416. Its table is
`allwinner,sun50i-operating-points`, whose driver (disassembled from the
stock 32-bit ARM kernel at 0xc074f808) reads the `speed` nvmem cell,
assembles it little-endian with no shift or mask, and formats it with
"0x%8.8x" as the OPP prop name so the core looks up opp-microvolt-<key>.
That cell is vf-table@00, reg = <0x00 0x02> -- two bytes. Every key in the
stock CPU table exceeds 0xFFFF, so none can ever match, every die falls
back to plain opp-microvolt, and there 1416 MHz reads <0x00>: no voltage.
The vendor's top usable point is 1392 MHz at 1100 mV.

Our 480-1296 MHz voltages match the vendor's exactly and are untouched.

Worth recording why this was not caught when DVFS was brought up: 1416 MHz
was qualified with a 2-minute 4-core CPU load that peaked at 68 C. The
failing workload is tens of minutes of GPU + display + scanout at 78 C.
The DMM voltage measurements were correct; the frequency point was never
in the vendor's table to begin with.

Validated on the BUILT kernel, not merely as a runtime sysfs cap. The GPU
display arm ran 45 min / 2707 s with deaths=0, gpu_delta=1,316,959 and
afbd_delta=161,473, zero crash patterns on the console, 90 heartbeats with
no gaps, and the board up 52 minutes without a reboot
(docs/reference/expS-0055-validation-45min-clean.log). Both validity gates
hold: khz= shows 1104/1200/1296 and never 1416, so transitions were
happening and the ceiling was genuinely in force, and afbd_rate held 59/s
throughout, so the panel was driven for the whole run rather than the soak
going quietly idle. That is 3.2x the longest crash latency ever recorded
for this bug (848 s) and ~30-65x the latency this configuration used to die
at. VA-API decode is still 5/5 bit-exact on the same kernel.

1392 MHz at 1100 mV would recover most of the lost headroom and is what
the vendor ships, but it is untested on this board and is deliberately not
added here.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -216,11 +216,37 @@
 			clock-latency-ns = <244144>; /* 8 32k periods */
 		};
 
-		opp-1416000000 {
-			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1416000000>;
-			opp-microvolt = <1100000>;
-			clock-latency-ns = <244144>; /* 8 32k periods */
-		};
+		/*
+		 * NO 1416 MHz OPP. It corrupts memory under sustained load, and
+		 * the vendor does not use this point at any voltage.
+		 *
+		 * The stock table (allwinner,sun50i-operating-points) gives 1416
+		 * a nonzero voltage only under efuse keys that can never be
+		 * selected -- the `speed` cell is vf-table@00, 2 bytes, so every
+		 * key above 0xFFFF is unreachable and every die falls back to
+		 * plain opp-microvolt, where 1416 reads <0x00>. The vendor's top
+		 * usable point there is 1392 MHz at 1100 mV.
+		 *
+		 * Measured on the bench with mpv rendering on the GPU to the
+		 * panel (tools/video/soak-display-only.sh, docs/vaapi-scope.md):
+		 *   ceiling 1416  -> kernel corruption in 40-90 s, n=3
+		 *   ceiling 1296  -> 20 min clean, 581k GPU IRQs, 1296 exercised
+		 *   ceiling 1200  -> 20 min clean
+		 * The victim differed every time -- a task_struct's cpu_context.sp,
+		 * a pool_workqueue, the CCU's ioremap pointer -- with no IOMMU or
+		 * GPU MMU fault anywhere, which is what bit flips look like and is
+		 * why this was chased through cedrus, the IOMMU, panfrost and CMA
+		 * before landing here.
+		 *
+		 * 1416 was originally qualified by a 2-minute 4-core CPU load at
+		 * 68 C. The failing workload is tens of minutes of GPU + display
+		 * at 78 C. The DMM voltages were right; the frequency point was
+		 * never in the vendor's table.
+		 *
+		 * 1392 MHz at 1100 mV is the vendor's top default and would be the
+		 * obvious way to claw back the last 7%, but it is UNTESTED here --
+		 * do not add it without its own soak.
+		 */
 	};
 
 	timer {
